      "claim": "Cefpirome achieved a mean peak CSF concentration of 10.8 µg/ml after IV dosing in children with bacterial meningitis, indicating favorable CSF penetration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C06"],
      "claim": "Cefotaxime showed excellent CSF penetration in patients with bacterial meningitis, with trough CSF concentrations of 6.9 mg/l on day one of therapy.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C14"],
      "claim": "Cefpodoxime showed low CSF penetration of about 5% in piglets, providing a negative reference among structurally similar cephalosporins.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "Most closely related cephalosporins with direct human CSF evidence demonstrate meaningful CNS access during meningitis, supporting a pass prediction. The single negative cefpodoxime result is outweighed by consistent positive CSF penetration data from cefpirome, cefotaxime, and related agents.",
